WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:02.129
One of my lifelong goals

00:00:02.129 --> 00:00:07.950
is to find an Earth sized planet in an
Earth like orbit about a sun like star.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.234
One that has a thin atmosphere,
and that has water oceans.

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:15.339
One where we can we can 
point to a specific star

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:19.910
and say that star has a planet like Earth.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.779
What I love to dream about is maybe 
there's some life on that planet

00:00:22.779 --> 00:00:27.328
you know, looking back at our Earth and 
our star and saying the same thing.

00:00:27.328 --> 00:00:28.660
Like how amazing would that be?

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.113
It would be amazing.

00:00:35.838 --> 00:00:39.908
Now, this idea - that the stars have 
planets of their own

00:00:39.908 --> 00:00:42.860
and that those planets might have 
aliens living on them

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.350
has been around for centuries.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.611
At first it had some pretty harsh critics.

00:00:48.611 --> 00:00:51.142
But by 20th century, it was mainstream.

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:55.336
Alien worlds showed up in books 
and then movies and TV.

00:00:55.336 --> 00:01:01.278
"In this galaxy, there's a mathematical probability
of three million Earth-type planets."

00:01:04.189 --> 00:01:06.923
But here’s the thing - even after all that,

00:01:06.923 --> 00:01:12.417
even when this episode of Star Trek: The Next
Generation aired in 1987

00:01:12.417 --> 00:01:19.444
scientists still hadn’t confirmed the existence
of a single planet outside our solar system.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:27.915
For all we knew, these so-called “exoplanets”
could only be found in fiction.

00:01:27.915 --> 00:01:30.251
But the next generation of astronomers,

00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:33.740
armed with new telescopes and 
more powerful computers,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.426
set out to change that.

00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:36.777
This is Sara Seager.

00:01:36.777 --> 00:01:39.336
She’s a professor of astrophysics at MIT.

00:01:39.336 --> 00:01:42.061
It's the spirit of us being the first explorers,
you know.

00:01:42.061 --> 00:01:44.550
We get to see what's out there 
for the very first time.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.314
We get to know if there are 
other planets like ours.

00:01:47.314 --> 00:01:50.270
Here’s a map of what they’ve found so far:

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:55.530
It's our night sky, which is a sphere 
projected onto this square map.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.369
It's sort of one of the maps of the world,
it's like, well,

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:00.689
obviously we're on a globe, 
but this is all stretched out.

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.689
Exactly, it’s all stretched out

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:05.733
The black dots are visible stars with 
the North Star, Polaris

00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:07.530
up here at the top of the map.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:11.709
And this band is the densest part of our own
Milky Way Galaxy.

00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:14.258
The lines trace out constellations.

00:02:14.258 --> 00:02:17.610
So we can look for a familiar constellation
like the Big Dipper.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.261
Up here.

00:02:18.261 --> 00:02:19.749
And here's Orion, actually.

00:02:20.969 --> 00:02:26.159
And these colorful dots mark the position
of stars with known “exoplanets”.

00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:28.854
The very first exoplanet around
a sun-like star

00:02:28.854 --> 00:02:31.840
wasn’t discovered until 1995.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.000
It was called 51 Pegasi b.

00:02:35.648 --> 00:02:37.370
And this was an incredible planet.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:38.739
It's astonishing.

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:40.413
It's about a Jupiter mass.

00:02:40.413 --> 00:02:43.180
But the time that it takes 
to go round it star

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:47.530
remember Jupiter takes 12 years,
51 peg takes four days.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.161
So it's year is four days long.

00:02:49.161 --> 00:02:51.218
Yeah, I don't think you could 
celebrate New Year's.

00:02:59.465 --> 00:03:02.874
But on 51 Peg b you’d have other things
to worry about.

00:03:02.874 --> 00:03:05.230
The planets in our solar system 
that are about its size

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:06.796
aren’t great places to live.

00:03:06.796 --> 00:03:08.764
They’re mainly just hydrogen and helium.

00:03:08.764 --> 00:03:10.366
There’s no surface.

00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:12.879
It just gets hotter and hotter and 
higher and higher pressure

00:03:12.879 --> 00:03:15.489
and eventually we would melt 
and we would be crushed.

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:17.470
Plus it’s really close to its star

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.761
much, much closer than even 
Mercury is to our sun.

00:03:20.761 --> 00:03:24.102
So it’s likely as hot as molten lava.

00:03:26.665 --> 00:03:28.105
Great, what else we got?

00:03:28.105 --> 00:03:31.299
After all this dreaming for millennia, 
it turns out

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:35.279
our very nearest star to our sun has a planet.

00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:37.470
And that is Proxima Centauri.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.235
About an earth mass planet, we don't know
the mass exactly

00:03:40.235 --> 00:03:43.530
and it's in the habitable zone of that star.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.870
The Goldilocks zone or the habitable zone

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.769
has the right temperature for liquid water.

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:50.593
It's not too hot.

00:03:50.593 --> 00:03:52.196
Not too cold.

00:03:52.196 --> 00:03:53.799
But just right for life.

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.400
The hotter the star the further away 
you've got to be.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.139
Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf 
much cooler than our sun

00:04:01.139 --> 00:04:03.842
so you've gotta be really close to stay warm.

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:07.909
When a planet is that close to its star, 
its rotation becomes locked.

00:04:07.909 --> 00:04:11.010
Meaning that one side of the planet 
always faces toward the star

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.615
one always faces away.

00:04:12.615 --> 00:04:14.071
That’s what happened to our moon.

00:04:14.071 --> 00:04:16.381
That’s why we see only one side of it.

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:19.100
If could visit that planet, 
the sun or the star

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.070
would be in the same place in the sky 
at all times.

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:24.828
So basically, yeah, there'd be a line 
on the world 

00:04:24.828 --> 00:04:29.420
-where it would always be sunset or sunrise, essentially.
-Exactly.

00:04:31.048 --> 00:04:33.463
-What's going on with this giant blob over here?
-It's amazing.

00:04:33.463 --> 00:04:36.650
This blob is where NASA's Kepler space telescope

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:39.769
stared at for about four whole years.

00:04:39.769 --> 00:04:46.160
“Two, engine Start, one, zero! And liftoff 
of the Delta 2 rocket with Kepler.”

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.040
The Kepler Space Telescope 
found thousands of exoplanets

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.822
by focusing on one patch of sky.

00:04:51.822 --> 00:04:54.455
Here’s all the discoveries made 
by other telescopes.

00:04:54.455 --> 00:04:56.808
And here’s Kepler’s contribution.

00:04:56.808 --> 00:04:58.790
So back here in the Kepler field

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.151
there's a really interesting planet called
Kepler 16 B.

00:05:03.151 --> 00:05:06.251
It was the first one where one planet 
is orbiting two stars.

00:05:06.251 --> 00:05:11.545
Sometimes science fiction is right - Tatooine.

00:05:11.545 --> 00:05:14.128
But in this case, it's a giant exoplanet.

00:05:14.128 --> 00:05:16.630
So once again, probably not one where 
we'd be walking around

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:17.924
and talking to Obi Wan Kenobi or anything.

00:05:17.924 --> 00:05:23.470
No, but still, it is supposedly in or near
the habitable zone of Kepler 16.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.360
So people like to see if it has a moon.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:27.681
That moon actually could be habitable.

00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:30.550
Imagine the sky there, lit by two suns...

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.360
...Two shadows, two sunsets...

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.329
...and dominated by a massive planet.

00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:41.590
Okay, let's look for pretty much 
the favorite exoplanet system right now.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.960
-Great.
-It's called TRAPPIST-1.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.625
It has seven planets around it.

00:05:45.625 --> 00:05:47.951
And they're all around about Earth mass.

00:05:47.951 --> 00:05:51.468
So you were talking about Mercury before 
as sort of a reference point

00:05:51.468 --> 00:05:53.431
for the distance to the star: 
do you know how far...

00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:57.009
Yeah, all these planets would be interior
to what would be Mercury's orbit.

00:05:57.009 --> 00:06:00.278
-It’s a very cute sort of planetary system.
-Cute and they’re all lined up in a plane.

00:06:00.278 --> 00:06:02.210
Y'know if we were on one of these planets

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.590
we could see the other planet 
in the sky would be

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.253
like bigger than our moon or something.

00:06:06.253 --> 00:06:08.118
And probably moving then.

00:06:08.118 --> 00:06:13.784
You would look up and see a moon
shooting across your sky.

00:06:13.784 --> 00:06:18.362
Scientists think that planets E and F 
fall in that sweet, sweet Goldilocks zone.

00:06:18.362 --> 00:06:23.860
But even if that’s off, some of these planets
must be the right temperature for life.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.318
The James Webb Space Telescope...

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:27.969
Which launched on Christmas morning 2021.

00:06:27.969 --> 00:06:31.029
...it has targeted every planet 
in the Trappist system.

00:06:31.029 --> 00:06:32.715
So we’ll be learning a lot more about it.

00:06:32.715 --> 00:06:35.620
And what’s going to be different about that
versus what we’ve seen before?

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.343
The James Webb will be much more powerful,
it has a bigger collection area.

00:06:39.343 --> 00:06:44.046
And it'll be orbiting very far from Earth
where the environment is just quieter and darker.

00:06:44.046 --> 00:06:46.240
Plus the telescope can see infrared light

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.330
which will make it easier
to study exoplanet atmospheres.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.060
This is where the science seems to be headed:

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.890
fewer surveys of broad swaths of sky,
more targeted investigations

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:00.299
of nearby stars and the atmospheres of planets
we’ve already found

00:07:00.299 --> 00:07:03.840
to see if any of our neighbors 
might support life.

00:07:06.368 --> 00:07:10.115
You know, there's sort of a habitable zone
for marshmallows.

00:07:10.115 --> 00:07:14.183
Actually there really is and it’s down here
right by the coals.

00:07:16.395 --> 00:07:18.431
Okay, here we go.

00:07:19.582 --> 00:07:21.408
Oh yeah.

00:07:22.686 --> 00:07:25.597
So we’ve found like 4,000?

00:07:25.597 --> 00:07:26.776
Yeah, thousands.

00:07:26.776 --> 00:07:29.231
Thousands of planets.

00:07:29.231 --> 00:07:31.798
What has been most exciting to you?

00:07:31.798 --> 00:07:32.659
It's everything!

00:07:32.659 --> 00:07:37.050
I mean, it's the sheer surprise,
like we'll find planets we had no idea existed.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:40.742
In our nice familiar system,
we’ve got a handful of petite rocky planets.

00:07:40.742 --> 00:07:43.608
And a handful of massive fluffy giants.

00:07:43.608 --> 00:07:45.102
And nothing in between.

00:07:46.131 --> 00:07:47.530
But out in the rest of the galaxy

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:53.495
the most common planets we’ve found are
big, dense, rocks nicknamed Super Earths.

00:07:53.495 --> 00:07:56.770
And big, less dense “Sub-Neptunes.”

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:01.510
To me what's the most mysterious is
our solar system is not incredibly common.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:03.989
The surveys have been going on for long enough

00:08:03.989 --> 00:08:06.120
that they should have seen 
lots and lots ofJupiters

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.700
but they’ve maybe only found one or two
that resemble ours.

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:14.265
And still no sign of a true Earth twin.

00:08:14.265 --> 00:08:16.316
Do you think that’s out there?

00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:18.575
It's definitely out there somewhere!

00:08:18.575 --> 00:08:19.490
The question is:

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:23.900
is one around a near enough star that
the telescopes we're trying to build

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:25.494
can actually find?

00:08:25.494 --> 00:08:28.654
Even if we do find that though,

00:08:28.654 --> 00:08:32.599
it won’t be for a long time that we're
ever able to visit or communicate.

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:34.052
And maybe never.

00:08:34.052 --> 00:08:35.455
So what’s the point?

00:08:35.455 --> 00:08:37.645
People always ask me, why are we doing this?

00:08:37.645 --> 00:08:40.829
Why are we searching for other planets?

00:08:40.829 --> 00:08:43.372
Why do we want to find another Earth?

00:08:43.372 --> 00:08:45.482
It's human nature to explore.

00:08:45.482 --> 00:08:47.478
We want to know what's out there.

00:08:47.478 --> 00:08:51.203
We've surveyed all the land on Earth, 
most of the oceans.

00:08:51.203 --> 00:08:54.865
And what's out there now it's outer space.

00:08:54.865 --> 00:08:57.060
We want to try to understand, 
why are we here?

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.290
How did our Earth come to be?

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.828
And finding other worlds like our own is a
step in that direction.

